<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Hardened-runtime entitlements for the macOS build of Nesq Bot.

  Apple's notary service REJECTS a bundle that is not built with the hardened runtime,
  and the hardened runtime in turn blocks what a WKWebView must do to run JavaScript at
  all. So this file is not an optional extra; without it the app either fails
  notarisation or launches to a window that never finishes loading.

  Note what is deliberately ABSENT: com.apple.security.app-sandbox. This app is
  distributed directly (Developer ID + notarisation + DMG), not through the Mac App
  Store, so it is not sandboxed. Adding the sandbox key would require entitlements for
  every file and network operation and is a separate piece of work — see
  docs/mobile-release.md.

  Also deliberately absent: any keychain entitlement. `src/secrets.rs` stores the Entra
  refresh token and the session JWT as generic passwords in the user's login keychain.
  A non-sandboxed app needs no entitlement for that — keychain-access-groups exists to
  SHARE items between apps or to reach the keychain from inside the sandbox, and this
  app does neither. What does matter is the signing identity: keychain items are ACL'd
  to the code that created them, so an app re-signed with a different identity (an
  unsigned local build vs. a Developer ID build) prompts the user once to allow access
  rather than reading the item silently.

  The build is wired up in .github/workflows/macos.yml — Tauri embeds this file into
  the signature via bundle.macOS.entitlements, and the workflow asserts afterwards that
  it actually made it in.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
  <dict>
    <!-- Outbound HTTPS/WSS to the Nesq Bot API.

         Strictly speaking this is an App Sandbox key and has no effect on a
         non-sandboxed hardened-runtime app, which already has unrestricted outbound
         network access. It is kept because it is what Apple and Tauri both put in the
         template, it costs nothing, and it is the one entitlement that would have to
         be correct on day one if this app ever moves to the Mac App Store. Do not read
         its presence as proof that network access is being granted by it. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- JavaScriptCore's JIT. This one is load-bearing: the hardened runtime denies
         writable-executable pages by default and WebKit needs them. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- WebKit also maps unsigned executable memory outside the JIT region. Broader
         than allow-jit and Apple's notary log will mention it, which is not a
         rejection. Required in practice for WKWebView under the hardened runtime; if a
         future WebKit stops needing it, dropping this key is the first tightening to
         try. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
    <true/>
  </dict>
</plist>
